course: medicine
lesson: 73
title: Trampolines, playgrounds and skate parks
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Ruby's four cousins pile onto the garden trampoline at once, «Get on, all of yous, it's way more craic!» («craic» is Irish for fun), and little Ellie keeps getting flung sideways: when several people bounce, one lands while the mat flies up, and the smallest gets thrown hardest.
explain: A trampoline throws you up, and you come down from wherever it threw you: onto the frame, the fence, the patio, or onto your own neck if a flip goes wrong, and a broken neck can mean never walking again.
explain: Playgrounds have quiet rules too: slides one at a time and feet first, walk wide around moving swings, touch a metal slide on a hot day before you sit, and take your bike helmet off on the climbing frame, because it can catch in the bars and the strap can press on the throat.
explain: Skate parks run on a queue nobody writes down: one rider in the bowl at a time, look before you drop in, and wear a helmet and wrist guards, because in a fall your hands shoot out by reflex and wrists take the hit.
explain: Saoirse put her hands out falling off her scooter and her wrist went «all wonky», which is what a broken wrist can look like, and a broken arm takes about six weeks to heal: no clip is worth that, so new tricks are learned step by step, not for the camera.
fact: Most trampoline injuries happen when more than one person is on it, and RoSPA (the Royal Society for the Prevention of Accidents) advises one person at a time.
fact: When several people bounce, the smallest child is usually thrown hardest and hurt most.
fact: Flips and somersaults on a trampoline can cause neck injuries; they should only be learned with a trained coach.
fact: A trampoline should have a safety net and frame pads, with space around it on soft ground, away from fences and patios.
fact: Trampoline parks have one person per bed and marshals to stop collisions.
fact: Slides are made for one person at a time, feet first; head-first, your face and neck reach the ground first.
fact: Metal and dark surfaces in strong sun can get far hotter than the air and burn bare skin.
fact: A moving swing is heavy and fast at a child's head height and can knock you out or break teeth, so walk wide around swings.
fact: Bike helmets should come off on play equipment because they can catch between bars and the strap can press on the throat.
fact: At skate parks one rider goes in the bowl at a time, and you look before you drop in.
fact: Putting your hands out when you fall is a reflex, so broken wrists are among the most common skate park injuries; everyone falls sometimes, even the best riders, and wrist guards and a helmet help.
fact: A broken arm takes about six weeks to heal.
fact: Showing off for a camera makes people take bigger risks, not make better landings.
fact: «Drongo» is Australian for a silly person; «arvo» is Australian for afternoon; a «toque» is a Canadian woolly hat.
exercise: How many people should be on a garden trampoline at once? | One | Two, if they're small | Four at most | As many as fit
exercise: Why do several bouncers cause injuries? | Bounces clash and the smallest gets thrown hardest | The springs get tired and stop working | Bigger kids sink through the mat | The net pulls everyone inwards
exercise: Leo wants to land a backflip for the group chat. What's the rule? | No flips on the garden trampoline | Try it once with someone filming you | Only flip if the mat looks soft enough | Flip backwards, but never ever forwards
exercise: What can a bad landing on your neck do? | Break neck bones and stop you walking | Nothing, trampolines are soft | Just give you a stiff neck for a day | Make you taller
exercise: Ava's trampoline has no net and sits by the patio. What makes it safer? | A net, pads and soft space around it | Aiming carefully for the middle every time | Bouncing with shoes on | Putting it on the patio instead
exercise: At a trampoline park, how many per bed? | One | Two at a time | Four if friends | Any number
exercise: Ollie goes down the slide head-first with a mate on top. What's the rule? | One at a time, feet first | Two at a time, heads first | Any way, if you're quick | Only on small slides
exercise: Matilda yelps on a metal slide on a 35 °C arvo. Why? | Metal in the sun gets much hotter than the air | Slides give small electric shocks | Shorts make slides slippery | Hot air makes skin sting
exercise: What should you do before sliding on a hot day? | Touch the slide with your hand first | Run down it as fast as you can so it can't burn | Pour cold water on your legs and arms | Wear a bike helmet in case you slip off
exercise: Ishaan runs right behind a moving swing for his ball. What's the rule? | Walk wide around swings | Run fast and duck | Wait right behind it | Grab the swing as it comes
exercise: Charlie climbs the frame in his bike helmet. Why is that a problem? | It can catch in the bars and the strap press on his throat | It makes him slower on the climbing frame | Helmets get scratched on bars | It's against the rules to look cool
exercise: Jack drops into the skate bowl while two others are riding. What's the rule? | One in the bowl at a time; look first | Bigger kids go whenever there's a gap | Shout and then go anyway | Go fast so you don't crash
exercise: Saoirse falls and puts her hands out. Why do wrists break so often? | Hands go out by reflex and take the hit | Wrists are the weakest bone in the body | Scooters hit wrists first | Wrist guards make them break
exercise: What should you wear at the skate park? | A helmet and wrist guards | Just a cap | Nothing, pads are for beginners | Earbuds and sunglasses
exercise: Zain tries a new jump «for the camera». What does an audience do? | Makes you take bigger risks | Helps you land better and more safely | Makes the ground feel softer somehow | Slows down your fall a little bit
exercise: How long does a broken arm take to heal? | About 6 weeks | About 6 days | About 6 hours | About 6 years
test: «Get on, all of yous, it's way more craic!» What would happen to the littlest cousin? | She'd likely get thrown hardest and hurt | She'd bounce the highest and most safely | Nothing, little kids are bouncy | She'd make the trampoline safer
test: «a trampoline's soft, you can't really get hurt». Put him right. | It throws you up, and you can land off the mat or on your neck | It's soft, so nothing can go wrong | Only nets can hurt you | You can only get hurt with shoes on
test: Nisha: «it's a park, you can do what you want, innit?» What's true? | Parks are only safe if people follow the rules | Trampoline parks have no rules | The marshals are there to spoil it | Two per bed is fine if you're friends and careful
test: Matilda: «Last one down's a drongo!» (Aussie for a silly person). It's 35 °C. A metal slide can reach over 50 °C. How much hotter than the air? | 15 °C | 85 °C | 50 °C | 5 °C
test: In a cartoon a kid flies off a trampoline over the fence and lands on his feet laughing. What's real? | Landing off the mat causes broken bones | Kids always land on their feet | Fences are soft to land on | Trampolines throw you gently
test: Charlie: «helmet on means I'm safer, obviously». On the climbing frame, is he? | No, it can catch and the strap choke him | Yes, helmets keep you safe everywhere you go | Yes, if the frame is metal | No, because it gets too hot
test: Jack from Winnipeg, in his toque (a woolly hat): «it's my turn now, right?» while two are mid-run. What could happen? | A crash on hard concrete | Everyone moves out of his way | Nothing, the bowl is big | The others stop in mid-air
test: Saoirse: «me wrist, it's gone all wonky». What should happen? | An adult gets her checked; it may be broken | She shakes it off and keeps riding till home time | She waits a week to see | She puts a plaster on it
test: «pads are for noobs, pros don't fall». What's true? | Good riders fall too, so they wear pads | Experts never fall over once they've learnt | Pads make you fall more often, not less | Only beginners ever break their wrists
test: Zain: «film me, this'll go viral». A clip lasts 15 seconds; a broken arm about 42 days. Worth it? | No, a short clip isn't worth weeks in a cast | Yes, views make it heal faster | Yes, if the clip is really good | No, because clips have to be longer
test: Ishaan: «I'll be quick, it won't hit me». Why can't he dodge it? | It swings back heavy and fast at head height | Swings are too slow to hit you | Swings stop when kids are near | He could, if he ran in a zigzag and kept low
exam: Identify the principal recommendation of the Royal Society for the Prevention of Accidents concerning the use of domestic trampolines. | One person is to use the trampoline at a time | All family members are to use it simultaneously | Somersaults are to be practised without supervision | Nets are to be removed for safety
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hazard presented by a bicycle helmet worn upon play equipment? | The helmet may become trapped and the strap may constrict the throat | The helmet will invariably prevent all injury | The helmet causes the equipment to overheat | There is no hazard whatsoever
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning injuries sustained at skate parks. | Fractures of the wrist are among the most frequent | Injuries occur only among beginners | Protective equipment increases the frequency of falls | Wrists are never injured in falls
exam: It is given that the air temperature is thirty-five degrees Celsius on a sunny afternoon. Determine the appropriate precaution before using a metal slide. | The surface is to be tested with the hand | The slide is to be descended head-first | The surface is to be presumed cool | Bare skin is to be placed upon it directly
